{"reference":"Mark 9","book":"Mark","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Command to Keep Silent","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jesus tells Peter, James, and John not to report what they saw until after the Son of Man has risen (v. 9). Mark repeatedly includes commands of silence around spirits, healings, and disciples, a pattern often called the “messianic secret.” Readers disagree about how best to explain that pattern, so the passage itself is clearer than any single explanation of why Mark presents it this way.","verse_refs":["Mark 9:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_009_ea9c72e526eb2eab8623983d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Misunderstanding and Service","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The disciples do not understand Jesus’ prediction that he will be killed and rise after three days, and they are afraid to ask. Their argument over who is greatest immediately follows. This is part of a recurring Markan pattern: predictions of Jesus’ suffering are followed by disciple misunderstanding and teaching about self-denial, service, welcoming the lowly, and leadership that does not dominate others. The contrast helps explain why Jesus answers their ambition by making service, rather than status, the measure of being “first.”","verse_refs":["Mark 9:30-37"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_009_938c3d4821845915d03824f8"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The Boy’s Affliction and Harmful Uses of the Passage","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Mark narrates the boy’s suffering and Jesus’ confrontation with an unclean spirit within ancient conceptual worlds that include hostile spirits and exorcism. The story should not be used to diagnose modern illness as demonic or to replace medical or mental-health care. Nor do Mark’s severe warnings about causing others to stumble authorize abuse, coercion, religious violence, medical neglect, dangerous exorcism, testing God, or other harmful practices.","verse_refs":["Mark 9:14-29","Mark 9:42-50"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_009_a0b253fd608f3480740bbd5a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading These Warnings Responsibly","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter’s accounts of hostile spirits and its forceful warnings about harm are not permission for practices that endanger, exploit, coerce, or neglect people. This matters especially when these passages are read alongside present-day illness or vulnerability: their language does not authorize replacing appropriate care with harmful religious action.","verse_refs":["Mark 9:14-29","Mark 9:42-50"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_009_c5260d9e01232f31c259b227"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}